    },
    {
      "id": "JbQd-IenSwI89ZDRoOG0P",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1760.1883727379522,
      "y": 3914.487352945048,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 2350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b01",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1589923310,
      "version": 2543,
      "versionNonce": 672611157,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1776661733298,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How can we cut down on the number of queries to the database?\n\n\nThe Problem\n→ Every top-K request hits the DB directly\n→ Top-K query is resource intensive\n→ Millions of requests → DB buckles under read pressure\n\nThe Opportunity\n→ Non-functional requirement grants a 1 minute grace period between a view event\noccurring and it appearing in results\n→ Results don't need to be real-time — they just need to be fresh within 1 minute\n→ This window is the opening for caching or precomputation\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Cache the Top K for each time window\n\n🔧 Approach\n\nCache Key Structure:\n\ntop-k:{window}:{truncated_timestamp}\n\n→ window = time period (e.g. 1hr, 24hr, 7d)\n→ truncated_timestamp = aligns cache entries to fixed boundaries (e.g. floor to nearest\nminute)\n→ Value = top K videos for that window\n\nCache Hit (Overwhelming Majority) ✅\n→ Return from cache (Zero DB load)\n→ Sub-millisecond response — well inside the 10s of milliseconds requirement\n\nCache Miss\n→ Compute top-K from DB\n→ Store result in cache with TTL of a couple of hours\n→ Return result to client\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\nThe Cache Expiry Problem\n\nTwo Problems on Cache Miss ❌\n\nProblem A — Thundering Herd 🐘\n→ Cache expires - flood of requests simultaneously hit the DB\n→ Partially solvable with request coalescing — only one request per server per time\nwindow goes to DB, all others wait for that response\n→ Reduces DB hammering but doesn't eliminate the latency spike\n\nProblem B — SLA Breach ⚠️\n→ Top-K DB query takes longer than 10s of milliseconds\n→ Every request waiting on a cache miss breaks the latency SLA\n→ Request coalescing doesn't help here — waiting requests still experience the full DB\nquery latency\n→ Requires a more sophisticated approach to solve\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Precompute the Top K for each time window\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\nPrecomputation via Cron Job ⏰\n\nHow It Works\n→ Cron runs on fixed intervals, precomputing top-K for each time window\n→ Results written to cache proactively — before any request needs them\n→ Top-K service only ever reads from cache — never touches the DB directly\nWhy This Solves Both Problems ✅\n→ Thundering herd — no cache misses to trigger a flood; cache is always warm\n→ SLA breach — cron runs ahead of expiry, so the cache is refreshed before it's needed;\nevery request hits a warm cache and returns in sub-millisecond time\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\nHandling Cron Failure Gracefully\n\nThe Risk\n→ Cron fails or falls behind → cache expires → no warm results to serve\n\nThe Fix — Extended TTL as Safety Net ✅\n→ Retain cache entries for a couple of hours beyond their expected refresh time\n→ If cron is late or fails, top-K service serves stale data rather than nothing\n→ Stale top-K results for a short period is far preferable to a complete outage\n\nOperational Concerns (Noted, Not Deep-Dived)\n→ Need monitoring/alerting on cron lag\n→ Need visibility into whether served results are fresh or stale\n→ These are real concerns but not the focus of this design — most interviewers won't\npush here\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How can we cut down on the number of queries to the database?\n\n\nThe Problem\n→ Every top-K request hits the DB directly\n→ Top-K query is resource intensive\n→ Millions of requests → DB buckles under read pressure\n\nThe Opportunity\n→ Non-functional requirement grants a 1 minute grace period between a view event occurring and it appearing in results\n→ Results don't need to be real-time — they just need to be fresh within 1 minute\n→ This window is the opening for caching or precomputation\n\n\n🟡 Good Solution: Cache the Top K for each time window\n\n🔧 Approach\n\nCache Key Structure:\n\ntop-k:{window}:{truncated_timestamp}\n\n→ window = time period (e.g. 1hr, 24hr, 7d)\n→ truncated_timestamp = aligns cache entries to fixed boundaries (e.g. floor to nearest minute)\n→ Value = top K videos for that window\n\nCache Hit (Overwhelming Majority) ✅\n→ Return from cache (Zero DB load)\n→ Sub-millisecond response — well inside the 10s of milliseconds requirement\n\nCache Miss\n→ Compute top-K from DB\n→ Store result in cache with TTL of a couple of hours\n→ Return result to client\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\nThe Cache Expiry Problem\n\nTwo Problems on Cache Miss ❌\n\nProblem A — Thundering Herd 🐘\n→ Cache expires - flood of requests simultaneously hit the DB\n→ Partially solvable with request coalescing — only one request per server per time window goes to DB, all others wait for that response\n→ Reduces DB hammering but doesn't eliminate the latency spike\n\nProblem B — SLA Breach ⚠️\n→ Top-K DB query takes longer than 10s of milliseconds\n→ Every request waiting on a cache miss breaks the latency SLA\n→ Request coalescing doesn't help here — waiting requests still experience the full DB query latency\n→ Requires a more sophisticated approach to solve\n\n\n\n✅ Great Solution: Precompute the Top K for each time window\n\n\n🔧 Approach\n\nPrecomputation via Cron Job ⏰\n\nHow It Works\n→ Cron runs on fixed intervals, precomputing top-K for each time window\n→ Results written to cache proactively — before any request needs them\n→ Top-K service only ever reads from cache — never touches the DB directly\nWhy This Solves Both Problems ✅\n→ Thundering herd — no cache misses to trigger a flood; cache is always warm\n→ SLA breach — cron runs ahead of expiry, so the cache is refreshed before it's needed; every request hits a warm cache and returns in sub-millisecond time\n\n\n⚠️ Challenges\n\nHandling Cron Failure Gracefully\n\nThe Risk\n→ Cron fails or falls behind → cache expires → no warm results to serve\n\nThe Fix — Extended TTL as Safety Net ✅\n→ Retain cache entries for a couple of hours beyond their expected refresh time\n→ If cron is late or fails, top-K service serves stale data rather than nothing\n→ Stale top-K results for a short period is far preferable to a complete outage\n\nOperational Concerns (Noted, Not Deep-Dived)\n→ Need monitoring/alerting on cron lag\n→ Need visibility into whether served results are fresh or stale\n→ These are real concerns but not the focus of this design — most interviewers won't push here\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pIPigoQ79kMTSulNiV_td",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2800.212109999402,
      "y": 3919.7426906008513,
      "width": 797.7952025196778,
      "height": 3325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
