      "updated": 1775940933537,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SSE — What the Browser Gives You Free 🎁\n\n→ Reconnection handled automatically by the EventSource object\n→ Browser retries with exponential backoff on dropped connections — zero custom logic\nneeded\n→ Last-Event-ID header sent automatically on reconnect — server knows exactly where\nto resume\n→ No reconnect logic to write or maintain\n\n\nWebSocket — What You Have to Build Yourself 🔧\n\n→ ws:// upgrade handshake is more complex than SSE's HTTP connection\n→ Must write your own onclose handler for dropped connections\n→ Must implement backoff manually\n→ Must track last received message client-side and handle server-side replay\n→ Must handle ping/pong keepalives manually to detect half-open connections\n\n\nTradeoff:\n\nWebSockets enable full-duplex (bidirectional) communication, which justifies the extra\ncomplexity when clients need to send real-time data too — but for a read-only comment\nfeed, SSE's free reliability primitives make it the clearly better fit; you'd be paying\nWebSocket's complexity cost for no benefit ⚖️",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "y3IyYkIO3YRdtySwaxj2P",
      "originalText": "SSE — What the Browser Gives You Free 🎁\n\n→ Reconnection handled automatically by the EventSource object\n→ Browser retries with exponential backoff on dropped connections — zero custom logic needed\n→ Last-Event-ID header sent automatically on reconnect — server knows exactly where to resume\n→ No reconnect logic to write or maintain\n\n\nWebSocket — What You Have to Build Yourself 🔧\n\n→ ws:// upgrade handshake is more complex than SSE's HTTP connection\n→ Must write your own onclose handler for dropped connections\n→ Must implement backoff manually\n→ Must track last received message client-side and handle server-side replay\n→ Must handle ping/pong keepalives manually to detect half-open connections\n\n\nTradeoff:\n\nWebSockets enable full-duplex (bidirectional) communication, which justifies the extra complexity when clients need to send real-time data too — but for a read-only comment feed, SSE's free reliability primitives make it the clearly better fit; you'd be paying WebSocket's complexity cost for no benefit ⚖️",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
